X:1
T:Hen Ladi Fowr Benfelen
T:A Buxom Blonde Lady
R:cân
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=16 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by John Thomas (born 1912), an engineer of Felin, Abercastell, Mathri, Pembrokeshire; recorded 10.8.63 (St Fagans tape 609). As a child he learnt it from hearing his uncle Ben Phillips, "Ben Bach" (1871-1958), the well-known folk singer, whose BBC recording had three more verses. Two songs seem to have merged here: verses 1-3 are "Hen Ladi Fowr Benfelen", a rare Welsh example of erotic song built on a craft metaphor (mowing the hay meadow, much like the English "Buxom Lass"), and the rest tell of a dream of the singer's own wedding and feast. The tune is a variant of the often-printed "Cân y Melinydd" (The Miller's Song).
N:The score shows how the other verses fit with small notes and a second voice; these are left out here. Verses 4, 5, 6 and 9 add a small B flat after the A before bar 1; verses 3-7 and 9-10 add a small B flat after the last A of bar 1; in bar 2 the rest and A can be quavers G (G) A; in bar 3 three quavers B flat (B flat) B flat come after "dod", and verses 2-10 split "gat-re o'r" with small notes G and B flat; the last quavers of bars 6 and 7 can be split into semiquavers (A B flat, G G). The third B flat in bar 6 is in brackets on the score. The slurs on "Hen" and under "ladd yr" are dotted. The E in bar 8 was sung as E, E flat or in between.
